# Anything that ends up executing on the node host MUST be stored with LF.
#
# This is not cosmetic. `infra/user_data.sh.tftpl` was committed with CRLF, so terraform rendered
# CRLF into user_data and the instance received a shell script whose every line ended `\r`. It has
# worked only because cloud-init happens to normalise the payload before running it — the copy at
# /var/lib/cloud/instance/user-data.txt is 252 bytes larger than the scripts/part-001 it actually
# executes, one byte per line. Nothing documents that behaviour as a contract.
#
# It is more than a latent risk now: user_data writes infra/dig-origin-cert.sh to
# /usr/local/sbin/dig-origin-cert, and systemd runs THAT file directly, twice a day, with no
# cloud-init anywhere in the path to clean it up. `bash` rejects `wait_for_state_device() {\r`
# outright, which is how this was found — the rendered-bootstrap test parses what the instance
# runs, and CRLF does not parse.
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.tftpl  text eol=lf
*.tf     text eol=lf
